WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Metal Mech, Man and Machine.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:08.420
It can save mankind, but beware, this machine has a mind of its own.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:18.420
Metal Mech, Man and Machine.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:31.720
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.820
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.080
And I'm Joe.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.860
You know, it doesn't really work to have the three of us here.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.680
Lately, it's been only two.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.300
So I was thinking one of us could be the man and one of us could be the machine.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.120
But I guess maybe one of us could also be the Metal Mech?

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.120
I could just be like the ground that you guys stand on.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.060
We'll just walk all over you?

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.380
Yeah. Metal Mech, Man and Machine.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.660
I'll say the title again to get away from beating up Sean.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.340
We don't want to beat up Sean this early in the episode.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.240
No. We have a whole episode to do that.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:15.060
True, true. Here we have, you know, an interesting plot line of stolen alien war tech that we're now using to try to fight back against the aliens.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:23.020
But I also feel like I've seen this mech before and not from like a previous existing Metal Mech IP.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.600
This looks like a direct ripoff of the mech from Robocop.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.240
Almost. Yeah. I do see that more.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.500
I was thinking of the AT-STs.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.580
Sure. I mean, that could work, too.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.920
But I really think it's almost like one one with the Robocop.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.200
Now that you say that, I definitely see the Robocop mech.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.720
Which is just like, I guess, like, what happened here?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.780
Were they pitching? We already got a Robocop game on the NES and I believe we're getting another one.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.240
So, like, were they pitching a Robocop game and then when they got rejected, they were like, well, we're still using the assets.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.940
It's legally distinct Robocop.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.040
Robocop doesn't use that mech.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.540
That's true. That's the enemy.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.720
Maybe they were pitching like a, you know, a spy versus spy situation where one person got to play as the opposition.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:11.160
Yeah. Or also, I guess, in this game's lore, the main character did steal the tech from the enemy.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.540
So, I guess that would fit Robocop.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:26.740
And I suppose, like, it would be interesting to get like a first level where you play as like finding this piece of machinery and like, you know, stealing it from the aliens.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.520
Like, it is just kind of like lame that it just like it happens in the manual and then you just start off with the machine.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:40.700
Because I guess to that, I would just say, why not just have the setup be that like the military has a brand new technology that's like not quite ready yet.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.480
But like, we're going to use it anyway.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.540
And that, you know, that explains it.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.320
It just feels like, I don't know, like, this is alien tech.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.260
It feels like it was built for this guy.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:58.100
Yeah. You're thinking in like, you know, game story design 10 years, 15 years into the future.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.580
That's a little too much.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.160
And if it was the year 2025, like it is in the game, ooh, imagine one year ago.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:17.780
You know, aliens, giant robots, like all that stuff sounds pretty futuristic for 1991 to jump all the way to 2025.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.660
But we're still just calling people regular names.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.280
The protagonist name is Tony.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.420
Come on. So we haven't, we haven't come up with any futuristic names.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.780
And we're just like, I suppose that's fine.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.680
That's probably the most realistic part of this whole thing is that we're still calling people Tony today.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.260
I mean, we are, but it would be more, it would be more realistic if his name was like Aiden or Brayden.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.880
That's true. Well, I mean, yeah, cause are there, how old is this character?

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.460
Because I feel like there are Tonys in their thirties, but there are no Tonys in their twenties.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.160
And that's a fact. That's a fact.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.820
Because they're called Anthony's now instead?

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.420
Or like, have we dropped the Tony nickname?

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.780
People stopped naming their kids Tony after 1995.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:07.960
I know that I went through like a time where like, you know, I would join a classroom that would have like nine other mics in it.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.040
And now I can't find another Michael.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.700
So I think that is a real problem.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.340
Well, what happened to the other mics that you grew up with?

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.380
No, they're still here, but we are going to leave the earth someday, Joe.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.680
I don't know how to break this to you, but like one day the, all the mics will leave.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.500
Have you met a Bob recently?

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.420
Uh, but I don't think they want to go as Bob.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.140
Well, sure, sure, sure.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.460
But some people are actually named Bob like on their birth certificate.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.340
Oh, that sucks. I'm sorry.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.100
I kind of like Bob.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.260
I wish there were more Bobs.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.820
You like Bob. Bobs are usually pretty nice.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.240
What about Bob? They all come back around.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.980
You know, I have a niece who's like best friends named Vivian and like Eustace or something.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.980
Eustace. Eustace is, yeah.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:05:01.100
No, it's this three little girls that sound like the golden girls and it cracks me up every time my brother talks about my four year old niece and her three, you know, seven year old friends.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:15.080
Yeah. And this game metal mech man and machine, you do play as both man and machine in the sense of, uh, from the blaster master approach where, uh, part of the game is played in the machine.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:23.100
And then eventually you'll need to hop out of the machine and be the little guy, uh, to do things that the mech can't do in the level in order to, uh, progress properly.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.940
And, uh, I'm sure we'll spend the rest of the episode talking about whether or not that works.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:33.680
But just to give a little bit more of an overview, this was developed by sculptured software.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.340
Um, I thought it was sculpture software, but there is a D at the end of that.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:44.660
So sculptured software, um, event, uh, formed in 1985, uh, based out of Salt Lake city.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.340
You know, this is the cutting edge information that most people want.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.620
This means I did the research.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.660
Uh, but they are, this is like one of the first works from them.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:56.580
Eventually they'd be responsible for those super star Wars games on the super Nintendo.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.780
I don't know if you guys ever saw those, but they're pretty cool games.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.480
Um, and they also did the, the difficult, right?

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:03.680
Yes, they are difficult.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.760
Yeah. And they also did the super Nintendo port of doom.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.100
Uh, which is also like a pretty neat thing.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.260
Yeah. It's neat.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.620
Yeah. Exactly.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:12.880
It's neat that it exists.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.720
Right. Uh, and Japan got this game first as metal flame.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:18.840
Psy buster.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.400
And we can debate whether that's a better name, but I also think it's interesting.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.180
Japanese translation to metal neck man.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.920
Yeah. Right. Metal metal flame.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.200
Psy buster is the English translation of the Japanese title.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:38.740
But I think what's more interesting is that means that this American company published

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.060
their game first in Japan.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.480
Well, you know, everything, everything was bigger in Japan at that time.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.900
Yeah. But it just, it's like, why not just like simul release it?

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.580
Right. And I'm making it in the States.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:49.580
It feels kind of weird.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:54.120
I understand it was published by Jaleco, but like, I can't think of too many instances

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.540
where that happens, where you're like, yeah, like we're an American company and we're going

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.360
to release it over on the Famicom first.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:02.960
See if it, see if it does well.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.240
That is a good point.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.440
Yeah. Is it kind of like how, isn't there like a Matt Damon movie that was really big in China

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.800
and never came here?

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:16.860
Wow. I'm, I, it's ringing a bell, but I also, I just feel like you could have said anything

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.560
about Matt Damon and I'd be like, yes, it's ringing a bell.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.840
Um, he is a person on earth.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.520
He is. Uh, metal flame side buster.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.680
Is that better than metal mech man and machine?

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.080
I think so.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.440
Yeah. I just like the idea of a metal flame.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.460
See, they have a different, they have a different vibe to me.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.580
Like the, the, the, just the phrase side buster, it just feels more like, feels like you're

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:40.420
going to be more agile.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.540
You're going to be more like a, like a ninja or you're going to be sneaky or something.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.100
Then metal, metal mech man and machine feels like really industrial to me.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:54.580
I do wonder though, if maybe just the, you know, I think at the time buster was kind of,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.980
uh, an insult.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.000
Um, so maybe they just didn't want to do that.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:05.060
And it was announced as mechanoids man and machine, uh, before becoming metal mech.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:06.800
So absolutely the worst of the three.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.820
Yeah. Mechanoids.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.540
It's going to be like little robotic gremlins or something running around.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.420
A little mechanoid.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:22.120
So yeah, the, the eject button is basically the, uh, the neat trick of the game.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:27.440
And unfortunately we've seen this trick before with blaster master, but you start out in,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.900
um, in the mech and at any point you can pop Tony out of the hatch and run around on foot.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.700
The mech is obviously and rightfully tougher, but, uh, like significantly tougher.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.140
Like this thing can take a lot of freaking damage.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:44.420
I would even had trouble keeping track of like, what is my health?

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:45.580
Like, is it on screen?

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.280
How many more hits can I take?

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.600
He just takes a, a freaking beating.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.800
And the problem is he also takes a beating when you're out of the mech.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.340
Yeah. That was not a feature in blaster master.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:02.160
I mean, give them credit for realism that like, it doesn't just because you get out of

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.540
it doesn't make it not a target.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:09.260
Um, it's kind of like, I guess getting your car stolen in grand theft auto or something.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.020
But, um, I was not expecting that.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:18.040
I also wasn't expecting the thing that you were going to have to do all the time to only

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.940
be accessible whilst menu diving.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.160
Um, you do have to go to a menu to get out of the vehicle.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:30.160
Yeah. Especially because it's the only thing in the menu that does anything of meaning.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.820
Well, unless you want to view the credits.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.600
There's the credits of the two people.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:34.940
And there's like three people in the credits.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.100
There's that you can turn the music that usually gets clipped anyway off.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:41.680
Yeah. You can't hear the music anyway.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.060
And if you're going to have three people in your credits and you really want to put

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.400
it on there where people can just have it on the screen, the whole game, just have it

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.320
at the bottom of the screen. Have a heads up display with the three people that made the game at the entire time.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.240
I just think it's the strangest placement for credits I've ever seen.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:09:59.760
Like put them on the main menu.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:03.780
Sure. Especially when there's only that many people, you probably fit them on, but you usually you could

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:08.280
have like, you know, start, continue, and then like options.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:09.600
And then where, inside options.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.820
Right. Yeah. But like to have it in the mech is like a very interesting placement because it's like,

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.800
at any given moment, is there like some kind of piracy test where like you have to be

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.620
able to check the credits of the game in order to like, is there ever an Easter egg

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.780
in this game that requires checking the credits?

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.980
You're, you're, it's like a Kojima game.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:35.180
Yeah. Right. Yeah. Well also it doesn't say credits.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:36.280
It just says metal mech.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.060
That's the button. That's so I'm like, Oh cool.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:39.120
I'll do something with the metal mech.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.320
It just plays the credits.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.360
Yeah. I guess that means to view the people that made the metal mech, but it should.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.600
Like, why would you think that?

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.500
Right. You shouldn't think that.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:55.080
And to Sean's original point, the weirdest part is, is that, uh, eject is the button that

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:00.120
matters here and you have to go into the menu every time to do it when they very clearly

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:04.760
could have just made it like select to choose between getting in and out of the mech.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.080
You know, it's really weird.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:14.340
I'm, you know, as we usually do, uh, I'm watching a video of a guy play the game while we talk.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.240
And I just, I'm watching him get out of the mech without having to go to the menu.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:20.660
I don't know how he's doing it.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.120
I thought I just saw that too.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.780
And I was like, Oh no, we are talking right now about how you can't do this.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:26.940
Oh my gosh.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.360
I'm looking at the manual to confirm you can do it.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:31.420
You ready? Oh my God.

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:33.260
But no, it's, it's harder than it seems.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.520
It's not just select it's select and the a button.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:39.420
Select a.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.280
Okay. Select and the a button.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:46.700
And now here's why you wouldn't think to do that because to return Tony back to the

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:47.800
mech. You just press select.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:53.480
So you wouldn't think like, Oh, there's probably some secret button string.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.600
I have, this is why we need Sam.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.620
We need somebody to read the manuals before this, but I still think our argument is,

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.740
is warranted. It's a really weird way to do it.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:10.580
Um, I think it was just a decision, uh, you know, based entirely on conjecture to, it

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.860
was just like, no, I made, I made this asset.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.300
I made the heads up to not heads up display.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:22.600
Like the, uh, the image of all of the bells and whistles on the dashboard of the

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.340
mech. And I need that to be in the game.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:26.320
And it was just the two of them.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:30.800
Um, so there wasn't really anybody saying that's a stupid idea in the room.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.680
So that's why it's there.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:38.360
And then, um, we were talking about how when you're out of the mech, it still takes damage.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:43.860
But the interesting part about that, uh, even if it is like a little realistic is that,

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:49.680
you know, in between levels, uh, Tony's health refills at the start of each level.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.040
But metal mech is that what we're, is metal mech the name of this machine?

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.900
Like, should I be calling him metal mech?

